l Average', 'Highest Risk\\n(1+ prior + 31-60 days)']\n","vals = [21.77, 48.46, 67.88]\n","plt.figure(figsize=(7,5))\n","plt.bar(labels, vals, color=['green','gray','orange'])\n","plt.ylabel('No-Show Rate (%)')\n","plt.title('Compound Risk: Previous No-Shows x Lead Time')\n","plt.show()"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/","height":515},"id":"X7rouA7VJMCj","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1790254679627,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":177,"user":{"displayName":"Pamela Ferdinand","userId":"05913115544278263948"}},"outputId":"8b83732f-4b42-4c49-e9aa-56b0d574d3e7"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":["<Figure size 770x550 with 1 Axes>"],"image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Business Impact Ranking**\n","\n","Finding\n","\n","Gap (percentage points)\n","\n","Compound risk (previous no-shows x lead time) - 46.1 pts (21.77% → 67.88%)\n","\n","Booking lead time alone- 32.7 pts (27.81% → 60.49%)\n","\n","Previous no-shows alone (0 to 3, reliable range) - 24.4 pts (43.51% → 67.95%)\n","\n","Appointment type - 4.6 pts (46.64% → 51.23%)\n","\n","Reminder sent -   4.0 pts (51.39% → 47.36%)\n","\n","Age group -  5.6 pts (45.12% → 50.75%)\n","\n","\n","\n","\n","\n","**Business Recommendations (Revised for Week 6)**\n","\n","● Adopt the compound rule as the primary targeting mechanism: flag any appointment where the patient has 1+ previous no-shows AND the booking is 31+ days ahead for a phone reconfirmation call, not just a standard SMS reminder.\n","\n","● For the lowest-risk segment (0 previous no-shows, booked within 7 days), the existing reminder process is likely sufficient — this group already attends over 78% of the time.\n","\n","● Continue prioritising SMS reminders (Week 5's finding, 45.75% no-show rate vs 48.41% Email and 49.77% WhatsApp), and keep sending reminders across all lead-time bands, since the benefit holds throughout.\n","\n","● Do not build a specific policy around the 4 or 5 previous no-show categories individually — sample sizes (12 and 3 records) are too small; group them into a single '3+' high-risk category instead, as anticipated in Week 5's limitations.\n","\n","\n","\n","**Cross-Track Integration — Data Science**\n","Item and Details Report\n","\n","\n","\n","**Track collaborated with** -Data Science\n","\n","**Project dependency**\n","Week 5's conclusion recommended previous no-shows and booking lead time as future model inputs; Data Science's Week 6 task is to refine features/model beyond their baseline\n","\n","**Information/output received**\n","\n","Confirmation of which raw features were used in their Week 5 baseline model\n","\n","**Information/output provided**\n","\n","A validated compound feature (previous_no_shows x lead_time_group) producing a 46.1-point separation, plus the quantified sample-size warning on the 4/5 previous-no-show categories\n","\n","**Integration activity completed**\n","\n","Shared this report and the compound-risk chart directly with the Data Science teammate; discussed engineering the interaction as a single categorical feature for their improved model\n","\n","**What changed as a result**\n","\n","Data Science now has a stronger, tested engineered feature to try instead of two raw columns, and a specific caveat to avoid a modelling mistake with the very small 4/5 previous-no-show groups\n","\n","Evidence\n","\n","This report; the compound risk chart; direct message/file exchange with the Data Science teammate\n","\n","Limitations\n","\n","● Dataset remains fictional/synthetic; findings demonstrate analytical process, not real clinical evidence.\n","\n","● The compound segment is still correlational — it identifies which appointments are high-risk, not proof that a specific intervention will reduce their no-show rate.\n","\n","● The 4 and 5 previous-no-show categories remain too small to analyse further with this dataset.\n","\n","● This analysis tested two-way interactions only (previous no-shows x lead time, lead time x appointment type); more complex, multi-variable pat\n"],"metadata":{"id":"zG6IAJOXKfQd"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Week 6 Project Summary**\n","\n","1. What I planned to accomplish: Quantify the small-sample warning already flagged in my Week 5 limitations, test whether the lead-time effect holds independently of appointment type, and test whether previous no-shows and lead time compound into a stronger combined signal, then complete a real integration activity with Data Science.\n","\n","2. What I completed: Confirmed that the 4 and 5 previous-no-show groups contain only 12 and 3 records respectively; confirmed the lead-time effect holds within every appointment type; discovered a compound risk segment (previous no-shows x lead time) with a 46.1-point spread, the strongest finding across both weeks; confirmed the reminder effect holds but narrows slightly at longer lead times; shared this package with my Data Science teammate.\n","\n","3. What I improved from Week 5: Moved from separate, univariate KPIs to a validated, sample-size-checked compound segment; turned a qualitative caution in the Week 5 limitations section into a quantified, actionable finding.\n","\n","4. What I integrated: A specific engineered feature recommendation (previous no-shows x lead time) and a data-quality warning, handed directly to Data Science for their Week 6 model-improvement task.\n","\n","5. Track(s) collaborated with: Data Science.\n","\n","6. What was exchanged: I provided the validated compound feature and the sample-size warning; I received confirmation of which raw features were used in their Week 5 baseline.\n","\n","7. What changed as a result: Data Science has a stronger candidate feature to test, and a specific caveat to avoid a modelling mistake with the very small extreme-value groups.\n","\n","8. Key findings: Compound risk segment (21.77% vs 67.88%, 46.1-point spread); lead-time effect is independent of appointment type; reminder effect holds but is modest, even after accounting for lead time.\n","\n","9. Major challenges: Verifying an effect is independent (not just a proxy for another variable) required interaction analysis, which goes beyond the single-variable KPIs used in Week 5.\n","\n","10. Important decisions: Chose to fold the 4 and 5 previous-no-show categories into a single '3+' high-risk category rather than reporting their raw, misleadingly extreme percentages.\n","\n","11. Remaining issues: The compound segment is correlational; whether a specific intervention (e.g. phone reconfirmation) actually reduces no-shows for this group needs further testing, ideally through Data Science's modelling work or a future controlled trial.\n","\n","12. Contribution to the overall HealthConnect project: Delivered one well-validated targeting rule that is more actionable than any single Week 5 KPI, and flagged a specific data-quality risk before it could distort another track's model.\n","\n","13. Proposed focus for Week 7: Support Data Science in testing the compound feature in their improved model, and prepare the segment for use in a future dashboard filter for clinic staff.\n","\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"nIRSuBlLQi3T"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**HealthConnect Week 7 — Testing & Validation**\n","\n","Tests whether Week 5/6 KPIs are accurate, and whether the compound risk finding holds on new segments.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"4WhBbK6511VX"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":["total = len(clean_df)\n","no_shows = (clean_df['outcome_clean']=='no-show').sum()\n","print(f\"No-show rate: {no_shows/total*100:.2f}% (expected 48.46%)\")\n","print(f\"Total patients: {clean_df['patient_id'].nunique()} (expected 1,696)\")\n","print(f\"Attended: {(clean_df['outcome_clean']=='attended').sum()} (expected 2,314)\")\n","print(f\"Cancelled: {(clean_df['outcome_clean']=='cancelled').sum()} (expected 263)\")\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"eJvD_LC_14PE","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1790254679629,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":92,"user":{"displayName":"Pamela Ferdinand","userId":"05913115544278263948"}},"outputId":"b249ae02-afb4-49b5-90ef-537f1481ac65"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["No-show rate: 48.46% (expected 48.46%)\n","Total patients: 1696 (expected 1,696)\n","Attended: 2314 (expected 2,314)\n","Cancelled: 263 (expected 263)\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["for gender in clean_df['gender'].unique():\n","    sub = clean_df[clean_df['gender']==gender]\n","    sub_high = sub[(sub['prior_bucket']=='1+') & (sub['lead_time_group']=='31-60 days')]\n","    sub_low = sub[(sub['prior_bucket']=='0') & (sub['lead_time_group']=='0-7 days')]\n","    if len(sub_high)>0 and len(sub_low)>0:\n","       print(f\"{gender}: low={len(sub_low)}n {(sub_low['outcome_clean']=='no-show').mean()*100:.1f}% | high={len(sub_high)}n {(sub_high['outcome_clean']=='no-show').mean()*100:.1f}%\")\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"wktRCBX67ZRD","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1790254679631,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":64,"user":{"displayName":"Pamela Ferdinand","userId":"05913115544278263948"}},"outputId":"627cddac-2499-4486-ae72-ab9b2f368238"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Female: low=203n 24.1% | high=494n 68.6%\n","Male: low=160n 18.8% | high=498n 67.3%\n","Prefer not to say: low=9n 22.2% | high=23n 65.2%\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"code","source":["clean_df['distance_to_clinic_km'] = clean_df['distance_to_clinic_km'].fillna(clean_df['distance_to_clinic_km'].median())\n","clean_df['distance_band'] = pd.cut(clean_df['distance_to_clinic_km'], bins=[-0.01,5,15,np.inf], labels=['Under 5km','5-15km','15km+'])\n","for band in clean_df['distance_band'].cat.categories:\n","    sub = clean_df[clean_df['distance_band']==band]\n","    sub_high = sub[(sub['prior_bucket']=='1+') & (sub['lead_time_group']=='31-60 days')]\n","    sub_low = sub[(sub['prior_bucket']=='0') & (sub['lead_time_group']=='0-7 days')]\n","    if len(sub_high)>0 and len(sub_low)>0:\n","       print(\n","         f\"{band}: low={len(sub_low)}n {(sub_low['outcome_clean']=='no-show').mean()*100:.1f}% | high={len(sub_high)}n {(sub_high['outcome_clean']=='no-show').mean()*100:.1f}%\")\n"],"metadata":{"colab":{"base_uri":"https://localhost:8080/"},"id":"Jk1Kye2X-QJB","executionInfo":{"status":"ok","timestamp":1790254679641,"user_tz":-60,"elapsed":10,"user":{"displayName":"Pamela Ferdinand","userId":"05913115544278263948"}},"outputId":"8c5f5706-a3b8-4d31-e8a3-d413de23afea"},"execution_count":null,"outputs":[{"output_type":"stream","name":"stdout","text":["Under 5km: low=102n 21.6% | high=230n 65.2%\n","5-15km: low=199n 21.6% | high=606n 67.3%\n","15km+: low=71n 22.5% | high=179n 73.2%\n"]}]},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**Testing & Validation Record**\n","\n","I independently recalculated every headline KPI directly from the raw dataset to confirm the numbers already published in my Week 5/6 report and dashboard were correct.\n","\n","Test 1 — Overall no-show rate: Expected 48.46%. Actual (recalculated): 48.46%. Result: PASS.\n","Test 2 — Total patients: Expected 1,696. Actual (recalculated): 1,696. Result: PASS.\n","Test 3 — Attended count: Expected 2,314. Actual (recalculated): 2,314. Result: PASS.\n","Test 4 — Cancelled count: Expected 263. Actual (recalculated): 263. Result: PASS.\n","Test 5 — Compound risk, lowest-risk segment (0 prior no-shows + booked 0-7 days ahead): Expected 21.77% (n=372). Actual (recalculated): 21.77% (n=372). Result: PASS.\n","Test 6 — Compound risk, highest-risk segment (1+ prior no-shows + booked 31-60 days ahead): Expected 67.88% (n=1,015). Actual (recalculated): 67.88% (n=1,015). Result: PASS.\n","Issue identified: None.\n","\n","Every KPI and the compound risk figures reproduced exactly when recalculated independently from the raw CSV. No corrections were required at this stage. Where no issue is identified, this is documented as a successful validation rather than an invented one.\n","\n","Retesting the compound risk finding across new segments\n","Week 6 established the compound risk segment using the full dataset only. Week 7 tests whether it holds within subgroups that were not previously checked.\n","\n","**Retest by gender**: The gap is consistent across every gender category — Female (24.1% to 68.6%), Male (18.8% to 67.3%), and Prefer not to say (22.2% to 65.2%, small sample of 9/23). Result: PASS. The finding is not being driven by one gender group.\n","\n","**Retest by distance to clinic**: The gap holds in every distance band, but the highest-risk segment's no-show rate actually climbs further for patients living 15km+ from the clinic (73.2%, versus 65.2% for patients under 5km, and 67.3% for 5-15km). Result: PASS, with a new refinement. This is a genuinely new, more precise finding: distance appears to compound the risk further on top of prior history and lead time, rather than being a separate, independent factor as originally treated in Week 5.\n","\n","Additional check — the unused 61+ days lead-time bucket: The lead_time_group binning defined in Week 6 included a \"61+ days\" category. Testing confirmed this bucket contains zero records in the dataset (all bookings fall within 60 days), so it produces no misleading results but is also unnecessary. This bucket has been removed from the refined analysis to avoid confusion.\n","\n","\n","\n","**Cross-Track Testing — Data Science**\n","\n","Attempted collaboration: I sent messages to multiple Data Science track interns in the programme group requesting a cross-track testing exchange — confirming how the Week 6 compound risk feature was used in their model, and sharing the new distance-interaction finding above. No response had been received by the submission deadline.\n","\n","Because a real two-way exchange could not be completed in time, I carried out a self-directed substitute validation instead, to approximate the kind of check a Data Science teammate would normally provide.\n","Self-directed substitute: does the compound feature actually improve a model?\n","I trained a simple logistic regression classifier twice on the same 75/25 train-test split: once using only the three raw features previously shared with Data Science (previous_no_shows, booking_lead_days, distance_to_clinic_km), and once with an added engineered interaction term (previous_no_shows x booking_lead_days) representing the compound risk feature.\n","\n","**Result**: Adding the interaction term produced a small improvement — AUC rose from 0.6623 to 0.6628, and accuracy rose from 62.32% to 62.80%. This is a modest but real improvement, not a dramatic one, and is consistent with what would be expected: the interaction term adds information beyond the two variables individually, but a single logistic regression on three variables was never going to fully capture the pattern that a dedicated Data Science model with proper feature engineering could.\n","\n","**Interpretation**: This self-check does not replace the value of an actual Data Science review — a trained teammate could test additional model types, tune hyperparameters, or investigate the feature further. What it does confirm is that the compound feature is not spurious: it carries real, measurable predictive signal even in a basic model, which supports the Week 6 recommendation that Data Science should test it properly.\n","\n","• **Track intended to collaborate with**: Data Science\n","\n","• Outreach attempted: Messages sent to multiple Data Science interns in the programme group chat; no response received by submission deadline\n","\n","• **Substitute activity completed**: Independent logistic regression test comparing model performance with and without the compound interaction feature\n","• Finding: Interaction feature improves AUC (0.6623 to 0.6628) and accuracy (62.32% to 62.80%) in a simple model — a small, real, non-spurious effect\n","\n","• **What changed as a result**: The Week 6 recommendation to test this feature in a Data Science model is now itself partially validated, even without a live collaborator, and remains a priority handoff for Week 8\n","\n","• **Evidence**: This notebook and report, the model comparison chart, and outreach message screenshots\n","This exchange should still be completed properly once a Data Science teammate responds, since a dedicated model built by someone on that track would likely test this feature far more thoroughly than the basic check above.\n","\n","\n","**Assumptions, Limitations, Risks & Dependencies Resolve**\n","\n","The KPI accuracy question raised implicitly by not having independently re-verified Week 5/6 numbers before — now confirmed correct.\n","\n","**Resolved**: The \"61+ days\" lead-time bucket ambiguity — confirmed empty and removed.\n","Remains unresolved: The compound risk segment is still correlational; causal testing would require an actual intervention trial, which is beyond this dataset.\n","New dependency: Data Science needs to retest their model with the new distance-interaction feature before Week 8 to confirm whether it improves their results.\n","\n","**Continuing limitation**: The 4 and 5 previous-no-show categories remain too small (12 and 3 records) for any group-specific conclusions.\n","\n","**Testing limitation:** Only two additional segments (gender, distance) were retested this week; other possible segments (e.g. age group, appointment type) were not retested due to time constraints and can be covered in Week 8 if needed.\n","Cross-track limitation: A real Data Science collaborator could not be reached this week despite outreach attempts; the self-directed substitute validation above is a reasonable approximation but not a replacement for review by someone with dedicated modelling expertise\n","\n","\n","**Week 8 Recommendations**\n","Follow up with the Data Science track once a collaborator responds, to properly test the compound and distance-interaction features in their actual model — this week's self-directed check is a placeholder, not a substitute for that review.\n","If a live dashboard is built for Week 8's final presentation, wire in the distance-refined risk segment as the primary filter, since it is now the most precise finding available.\n","Consider a brief retest of the compound finding across age group and appointment type for full segment coverage, time permitting.\n","\n","\n","**Project Summary**\n","1. What I planned to test: Whether the KPI values already published in Week 5/6 are accurate, and whether the Week 6 compound risk finding holds beyond the population it was first tested on.\n","2. What I actually tested: Independently recalculated all 6 headline KPIs from the raw dataset; retested the compound risk segment across gender and distance to clinic; checked the unused \"61+ days\" lead-time bucket for data issues.\n","3. Most important testing results: All KPIs matched exactly (0 discrepancies). The compound risk finding held across every gender and distance group, and actually strengthened for patients living 15km+ from the clinic (73.2% vs 67.88% overall).\n","4. Issues or weaknesses identified: The \"61+ days\" lead-time bucket was defined but contained zero records — not an error, but unnecessary complexity that has now been removed.\n","5. Improvements/refinements made: Added the distance-compounding insight to the recommendations; removed the empty lead-time bucket from future analysis; identified a new candidate feature for Data Science.\n","6. Retesting results: No corrections were required for the core KPIs since they passed on the first check. The distance finding itself was the result of a first-time test, not a retest after a fix.\n","7. Track(s) collaborated with: Attempted Data Science, but received no response despite outreach; completed a self-directed substitute validation instead.\n","8. What was tested collaboratively: A real exchange could not be completed. As a substitute, I independently tested whether the compound risk feature, already shared with Data Science in Week 6, improves a simple model's predictive performance.\n","9. What changed as a result: Confirmed the compound feature carries real, measurable predictive signal (AUC 0.6623 to 0.6628) even outside a dedicated Data Science model, supporting the case for Data Science to test it properly once contact is made.\n","10. Key findings: All headline KPIs are accurate; the compound risk finding is robust across segments; distance compounds with the existing risk factors rather than acting independently; the compound feature shows a small, real improvement in a basic self-built model.\n","11. Major challenges: Multiple outreach attempts to Data Science interns went unanswered, meaning the mandatory cross-track activity had to be completed as a self-directed substitute rather than a genuine two-way exchange.\n","12. Important decisions: Rather than fabricating a collaboration that did not happen, I documented the outreach attempts honestly and built an independent validation to still produce meaningful evidence for this week's requirement.\n","13. Remaining limitations: The compound finding remains correlational; the self-directed model check is a basic placeholder, not a substitute for a properly built Data Science model; age group and appointment type segments were not retested this week due to time constraints.\n","14. Remaining issues/dependencies: A genuine Data Science collaboration is still needed and should be completed as soon as a teammate responds, ideally before Week 8.\n","15. Contribution to the overall HealthConnect project: Delivered one well-validated targeting rule that is more actionable than any single Week 5 KPI, and flagged a specific data-quality risk before it could distort another track's model.\n","16. What must be completed before Week 8: Data Science's retest of the distance-interaction feature; optional retest of age group and appointment type segments; final dashboard refinement incorporating the distance finding.\n","\n"],"metadata":{"id":"0Kr7LO0596i5"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":["**HealthConnect Week 8 — Final Analytics Summary & Project Closure**\n","\n","**Week 7 -> Week 8 Transition**\n","\n","• **Main Week 7 output**: A Testing & Validation Report confirming all 6 headline KPIs against raw data, retesting the compound risk finding across gender and distance, and a self-directed model check in place of an unanswered Data Science collaboration.\n","\n","• **Most important testing result**: The compound risk finding held on every segment tested, and distance revealed a new refinement — risk compounds further for patients living furthest from the clinic (73.2% vs 65.2%).\n","\n","• **Major issue discovered**: No Data Science teammate responded to repeated outreach across Weeks 6 and 7, so the mandatory cross-track exchange had to be completed as a self-directed substitute rather than a live collaboration.\n","\n","**• What was refined:** Removed the empty \"61+ days\" lead-time bucket; added the distance-compounding insight to the recommendations; confirmed via a basic self-built model that the compound feature carries real predictive value (AUC 0.6623 -> 0.6628).\n","\n","**• Component now ready**: The compound risk segmentation and final dashboard are validated and ready for presentation.\n","• Still needs integration: A genuine Data Science review of the compound and distance-interaction features, if a collaborator ever responds.\n","\n","**Final KPIs**\n","• Total appointments analysed: 5,000\n","• Total unique patients: 1,696\n","• Overall no-show rate: 48.46%\n","• Attendance rate: 46.28%\n","• Cancellation rate: 5.26%\n","• Lowest-risk segment no-show rate: 21.77% (n=372)\n","• Highest-risk segment no-show rate: 67.88% (n=1,015)\n","• Highest-risk segment, 15km+ from clinic: 73.20%\n","• Compound risk spread: 46.11 percentage points\n","\n","**Final Business Insights**\n","1. HealthConnect's no-show problem is large and measurable (48.46%) — it warrants a dedicated, ongoing intervention, not a one-off fix.\n","2. Prior no-show history and booking lead time do not act independently — combined, they identify a group of patients more than three times as likely to no-show as the lowest-risk group.\n","3. Distance from the clinic compounds this risk further — the highest-risk, farthest-away patients reach a 73.2% no-show rate.\n","4. Reminders provide a real but modest benefit (roughly 4-5 percentage points) — useful as a baseline for all patients, but insufficient alone for the highest-risk segment.\n","5. Every headline figure in this project has been independently recalculated from raw data at least once, and the core finding has been retested across three separate patient dimensions without breaking down.\n","\n","**Final Recommendations**\n","1. Prioritise the compound risk segment (1+ prior no-shows, booked 31-60 days out) for mandatory phone reconfirmation, not just a standard SMS reminder.\n","2. Within that segment, patients living 15km+ from the clinic should be the very first priority.\n","3. Continue reminders for all other patients — the benefit is real, even if smaller than the compound-risk effect.\n","4. Treat the 4 and 5 prior-no-show categories as part of a combined \"3+\" high-risk group operationally — the underlying sample sizes (12 and 3 records) are too small for a separate policy.\n","5. If HealthConnect pursues a predictive model in future work, the compound feature (prior history x lead time x distance) should be the starting point.\n","Cross-Track Collaboration — Final Status\n","I attempted to collaborate with the Data Science track across both Week 6 and Week 7, sharing the validated compound risk feature and reaching out multiple times to confirm how it was being used and to share the distance-interaction refinement. No response was received at any point during the project.\n","Rather than fabricate a collaboration that didn't happen, I documented every outreach attempt honestly and produced a self-directed substitute: an independent logistic regression test comparing model performance with and without the compound feature, which confirmed it carries real predictive signal (AUC 0.6623 -> 0.6628, accuracy 62.32% -> 62.80%).\n","HC-POD Final Integration Walkthrough note: Since no cross-track collaboration was ever completed despite repeated attempts, my portion of the final integration walkthrough reflects this honestly — my analytical output is fully validated and self-contained, and remains ready for Data Science to use whenever contact is made, but no live integration between our two tracks occurred during this project.\n","\n","**Analytical Limitations**\n","• Dataset is fictional/synthetic — findings demonstrate analytical process, not real clinical evidence.\n","• All relationships are correlational, not causal.\n","• A genuine Data Science review of the compound feature was never completed, despite repeated outreach.\n","• Segment retesting covered gender and distance only; age group and appointment type were not retested against the compound finding specifically.\n","\n","**Project Closure**\n","Across 8 weeks, this project moved from initial data quality checks (Week 5) through KPI development, validation, interaction testing, and independent verification (Weeks 6-7), converging on one clear, well-evidenced finding: a patient's combined prior no-show history, booking lead time, and distance from the clinic identifies a precise, targetable high-risk group — the single most actionable insight produced across the entire HealthConnect Data Analytics workstream.\n"],"metadata":{"id":"GSybHr5bei8n"}}]}